ource":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Discrimination & Race Relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-ants-among-elephants-an-untouchable-family-and-the-making-of-modern-india-sujatha-gidla"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Gidla, Sujatha, 1963- -- Childhood and youth","sourceId":"source-open-library-ants-among-elephants-an-untouchable-family-and-the-making-of-modern-india-sujatha-gidla"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Gidla, Sujatha, 1963- -- Family","sourceId":"source-open-library-ants-among-elephants-an-untouchable-family-and-the-making-of-modern-india-sujatha-gidla"}],"publicationYear":2017,"pageCount":306,"summary":"\"The stunning true story of an untouchable family who become teachers, and one, a poet and revolutionary. Like one in six people in India, Sujatha Gidla was born an untouchable. While most untouchables are illiterate, her family was educated by Canadian missionaries in the 1930s, making it possible for Gidla to attend elite schools and move to America at the age of twenty-six. It was only then that she saw how extraordinary--and yet how typical--her family history truly was. Her mother, Manjula, and uncles Satyam and Carey were born in the last days of British colonial rule. They grew up in a world marked by poverty and injustice, but also full of possibility. In the slums where they lived, everyone had a political side, and rallies, agitations, and arrests were commonplace. The Independence movement promised freedom.","thumbnailUrl":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/14609585-L.jpg","renownProfile":{"knowsBook":true,"publicFame":47,"criticalRenown":72,"controversy":28,"llmAffinity":86,"affinityResidual":7.27,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.88,"criticalRenown":0.86,"controversy":0.7,"llmAffinity":0.97},"tags":{"craft":"voice","evidence":"personal-experience","stance":"moral-seriousness"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"experimentalSemanticProfil